#   * <module> The shortest walk that collects every key: three nested layers, each to be replaced natively
#
# A family of the reduction series: a non-trivial problem, at least LeetCode
# hard and laborious in C or C++, with a concise definition whose solution
# needs special data structures and a pattern recognised and replaced natively
# at every layer of nested subproblems. The problem
# is LeetCode 864, "Shortest Path to Get All Keys", on a grid without locks.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition in three layers, in continuation
# passing style so that a set is chosen once, at the head (a set passed as an
# argument would be chosen afresh at every use):
#
#     lengths b v t   = { equal v t (\k. k 0) {},
#                         b {} (\p. { \k. lengths p u t (\l. k (successor l)) | u a successor of v }) }
#     tour c v r k    = full c (k r)
#                         { member c i {} (lengths B v key_i (\d. at_least r d
#                               (tour (insert c i) key_i (difference r d) k) {})) | i a key }
#     program         = tour none start T (\r. difference T r)
#
# - the inner layer, lengths, is the set of the lengths of the walks of at
#   most b edges from v to t (the family of corpora/contest_path_lengths.pl);
#   replaced natively it is a breadth-first distance;
# - the middle layer, tour, chooses the next key among those not collected,
#   its collected keys a bit set (the attractors bit_set_member, bit_set_insert
#   and bit_set_full); replaced natively it is the dynamic programme over the
#   subsets of keys and the last key, with the subset a bitmask;
# - the outer layer is the budget arithmetic, the attractors natural_at_least
#   and natural_difference on unary numerals; replaced natively it is integer
#   arithmetic.
# The inner layer's members are boxes whose normal forms are \k. k d, d the
# length of a walk; walks of one length give boxes of one normal form, so
# the set grows with the number of walks until equal members are merged. A
# setting that reads a box as a datum of a one-constructor Scott type and
# merges equal members by value is left with one box per length, which is
# the breadth-first distance layer.
# A setting that replaces only the inner layer still interprets the middle
# one, and every boundary between a native and an interpreted layer converts
# the values: the family measures how far the compilation reaches.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. S(P) is the set of the totals of the walks from the start
# that collect every key in some order, each leg a walk of at most B edges,
# the total at most T: collect_keys_totals/6. The points take T the least
# such total, so S(P) is that one number, the answer; the near miss takes T
# one less, so S(P) is empty. PROOF, with ⟦·⟧, D, S and Th and
# Lemma 2b of gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For an order of the keys and a walk per leg with total s =< T,
#   choose, at each tour, the key's branch, and in lengths the members along
#   the leg's walk: finitely many steps reach difference T (T - s), the
#   numeral s, pure and normal, in ⟦P⟧ by Lemma 2b.2.
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term P reaches by a finite
#   sequence σ. The only sets of P are the sets written above and {}; σ
#   removes a set at the head only by a member, and {} never (a set heads no
#   redex, {} has no member). So a pure term is reached only through a full
#   bit set, every key's branch taken once (member refuses a collected key),
#   each leg's d a walk length (the inner layer's members are boxes of walk
#   lengths, by the argument of contest_path_lengths.pl), and at_least keeps r
#   a budget of T minus the legs so far. The pure term is difference T r, the
#   total s = T - r of a walk collecting every key, at most T.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - breadth-first distances: R. Bellman, On a routing problem, Quarterly of
#   Applied Mathematics 1958, doi:10.1090/qam/102435;
# - the dynamic programme over subsets: M. Held, R. M. Karp, A Dynamic
#   Programming Approach to Sequencing Problems, J. SIAM 1962,
#   doi:10.1137/0110015;
# - tabled evaluation, which keys the tour by the bit set, the last key and
#   the budget: W. Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996, doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
#
# READINGS AND SIZES: in the points' comment below (THE POINTS).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   THE POINTS: collect_keys_point(?Point, ?Rows, ?Columns, ?Keys, ?Slack),
#   the start the corner 0 and the budget the least total plus Slack. Sized
#   by a probe of 2026-09-30 (CPUs 0-7, the whole set under a 10 s
#   limit, the budget the least total): tabling(weak_head) reads the 3 by 3
#   grid with three keys in 463 ms and the 4 by 4 grid with four keys in
#   6751 ms, and is stopped at the 5 by 5 grid with four keys; weak_head is
#   stopped at the 3 by 3 grid (it reads the 2 by 3 grid with two keys in
#   6580 ms). The near miss takes the budget one short of the least total.
